 ('h2', 'The title tag'),
 ('p', 'The title tag is the single most important meta tag. It provides the clickable headline shown in search results, the name that appears in the browser tab, and the default title when the page is bookmarked or shared. Search engines weigh it heavily as a signal of what the page is about, and users decide largely on the strength of it whether to click. A good title is concise, accurately describes the page, includes the main keyword naturally near the front, and reads as something a person would want to click, not a stuffed list of terms. Aim to keep it within roughly 50 to 60 characters so it is not truncated in results.'),
 ('h2', 'The meta description'),
 ('p', 'The meta description is the short paragraph shown beneath the title in search results. It is not a direct ranking factor, but it is effectively ad copy for your page: a compelling, relevant description raises the click-through rate, while a missing or generic one wastes the opportunity. Write it as a clear, inviting summary that includes the main keyword and gives the searcher a concrete reason to click. Keep it to about 150 to 160 characters so it displays in full. If you leave it blank, search engines will generate one from the page, usually less persuasively than you could.'),
