 ('h2', 'How the tags work together'),
 ('p', 'The three families of tag serve overlapping but distinct audiences. The title tag and meta description target search results and are optimised for search intent. The Open Graph and Twitter tags target social sharing and are optimised for scroll-stopping appeal. They can differ, a title tuned for search keywords and an Open Graph title tuned for shareability, though many pages sensibly reuse the same strong title across both. A generator makes it easy to produce the full set consistently, filling in the repetitive parts and letting you focus on the wording that matters.'),
 ('tool', 'meta-tag-generator', 'Meta Tag Generator', 'Generate title, description and Open Graph meta tags for any page, ready to paste into your HTML, entirely in your browser.'),
 ('h2', 'Meta tags in a wider SEO picture'),
 ('p', 'Meta tags are one part of on-page optimisation. The page\'s URL should be a clean, readable <a href="../tools/slug-generator.html">slug</a>; the content itself should use its target keywords naturally, which a <a href="../tools/keyword-density-checker.html">keyword density checker</a> helps you verify without overdoing it; and the character limits on titles and descriptions are best hit precisely with a <a href="../tools/character-counter.html">character counter</a>. Together these make a page both understandable to search engines and appealing to the humans deciding whether to click.'),
 ('h2', 'Other meta tags worth knowing'),
 ('p', 'Beyond the title, description and Open Graph tags, a handful of other meta tags do quiet but important jobs. The viewport tag tells mobile browsers how to scale the page and is essential for responsive design, without it, a site renders as a shrunken desktop version on phones. The charset tag declares the character encoding so text renders correctly. The canonical tag tells search engines which version of a page is the definitive one when similar content exists at multiple URLs, preventing duplicate-content confusion. And the robots meta tag can instruct search engines whether to index a page and follow its links.'),
 ('p', "Most of these are set once and rarely thought about again, but getting them wrong causes real problems: a missing viewport tag breaks mobile layout, a wrong canonical tag can hide the page you actually want ranked, and a stray 'noindex' robots tag can quietly remove a page from search entirely. This is one reason a generator that produces a complete, correct set of head tags is valuable, it ensures the routine tags are present and correct alongside the title and description you are actively crafting. When you set up a page, it is worth confirming that the essential technical tags are in place and that the canonical and robots directives say what you intend, because these invisible instructions shape how the whole page is treated by browsers and search engines. The visible title and description get the attention, but the supporting tags are what ensure the page is even eligible to perform well in the first place."),
 ('p', "To recap: the title tag is your page's headline in search and the strongest on-page signal of its topic; the meta description is the persuasive snippet beneath it; and Open Graph tags control the preview card when your link is shared. Write each for its audience, accurate and keyword-aware for search, compelling and visual for social, keep them within their character limits, and generate the full set consistently. These small, invisible tags do an outsized amount of the work of turning a search result or a shared link into an actual visit."),
